                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1203,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 818,
                    "height": 876,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "pc_region_moves is a set of buttons that move and stretch pc_regions' current region.\n\nWire its outlet to pc_regions' 'command'. Each button sends the message on its label:\n\n  left / right   move the region along x, by pc_regions' 'steps' cells\n  down / up      along y\n  back / forward along z\n  extend x       stretch the region to both edges of the volume along x\n  extend y       the same along y: a footprint becomes a floor-to-ceiling\n                 pillar\n  extend z       the same along z\n  extend y -     stretch it along y one way only\n  extend y +     the other way\n  undo           take back the last change\n\nThe directions are the axes of pc_regions' grid. With a levelled camera y is up and z is away from the camera.\n\nIt is an ordinary button_set otherwise: relabel the buttons, change the messages they send or colour them in its options, and the patch keeps what you set. 'extend x -' or 'move 0 2 0' are as good as any of these.\n\nSYNTAX:\npc_region_moves\n\nEXAMPLE:\npc_region_moves\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nOne inlet per button: anything arriving presses it.\n\nmessage / width / uniform_width / height / flash_duration / label 1 ... (options):\nAs for button_set.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe pressed button's message.\n\nRELATED:\npc_regions, which these messages drive. button_set is the general node.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 810,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 860,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "pc_region_moves is a set of buttons that move and stretch pc_regions' current region.\n\nWire its outlet to pc_regions' 'command'. Each button sends the message on its label:\n\n  left / right   move the region along x, by pc_regions' 'steps' cells\n  down / up      along y\n  back / forward along z\n  extend x       stretch the region to both edges of the volume along x\n  extend y       the same along y: a footprint becomes a floor-to-ceiling\n                 pillar\n  extend z       the same along z\n  extend y -     stretch it along y one way only\n  extend y +     the other way\n  undo           take back the last change\n\nThe directions are the axes of pc_regions' grid. With a levelled camera y is up and z is away from the camera.\n\nIt is an ordinary button_set otherwise: relabel the buttons, change the messages they send or colour them in its options, and the patch keeps what you set. 'extend x -' or 'move 0 2 0' are as good as any of these.\n\nSYNTAX:\npc_region_moves\n\nEXAMPLE:\npc_region_moves\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nOne inlet per button: anything arriving presses it.\n\nmessage / width / uniform_width / height / flash_duration / label 1 ... (options):\nAs for button_set.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe pressed button's message.\n\nRELATED:\npc_regions, which these messages drive. button_set is the general node."
                },
